1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,884
Stand next to the wheel of a

2
00:00:01,884 --> 00:00:04,644
Caterpillar seven nine seven and you will

3
00:00:04,644 --> 00:00:06,664
not reach the top of the tire.

4
00:00:06,844 --> 00:00:08,632
You will not even reach the middle.

5
00:00:08,812 --> 00:00:11,643
Each tire is about four meters tall

6
00:00:11,643 --> 00:00:14,069
and weighs around five tonnes,

7
00:00:14,069 --> 00:00:16,254
as much as two family cars.

8
00:00:16,434 --> 00:00:19,110
The truck carries more than three hundred

9
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:21,134
and sixty tonnes of rock in one

10
00:00:21,134 --> 00:00:21,461
trip.

11
00:00:21,641 --> 00:00:25,090
Today, eleven parts of a giant mining

12
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:27,980
truck, and why each one exists.

13
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:31,383
These trucks never drive on public roads.

14
00:00:31,563 --> 00:00:34,034
They live inside open pit mines,

15
00:00:34,034 --> 00:00:37,122
climbing out of holes hundreds of meters

16
00:00:37,122 --> 00:00:39,284
deep, all day and all night,

17
00:00:39,284 --> 00:00:40,828
loaded to the limit.

18
00:00:41,008 --> 00:00:43,019
A mine can run a fleet of

19
00:00:43,019 --> 00:00:44,226
dozens of them,

20
00:00:44,226 --> 00:00:47,363
and every minute one stands still costs

21
00:00:47,363 --> 00:00:47,846
money.

22
00:00:48,026 --> 00:00:50,627
So every part on them exists for

23
00:00:50,627 --> 00:00:51,358
a reason.

24
00:00:51,708 --> 00:00:54,674
As always, every fact comes from a

25
00:00:54,674 --> 00:00:57,116
named source, manufacturers,

26
00:00:57,116 --> 00:00:59,123
mining safety agencies,

27
00:00:59,123 --> 00:01:01,478
and engineering references,

28
00:01:01,478 --> 00:01:04,095
all listed in the description.

29
00:01:04,275 --> 00:01:06,790
Let's start with the part that gives

30
00:01:06,790 --> 00:01:08,186
this video its name,

31
00:01:08,186 --> 00:01:08,815
the tire.

32
00:01:09,165 --> 00:01:11,435
Why is this tire taller than the

33
00:01:11,435 --> 00:01:13,634
man standing next to the truck?

34
00:01:13,814 --> 00:01:16,677
Because the tire is what carries the

35
00:01:16,677 --> 00:01:18,188
load to the ground,

36
00:01:18,188 --> 00:01:20,336
and the ground in a mine is

37
00:01:20,336 --> 00:01:21,767
soft, broken rock.

38
00:01:21,947 --> 00:01:24,515
A Caterpillar seven nine seven,

39
00:01:24,515 --> 00:01:28,159
loaded, weighs more than six hundred tonnes.

40
00:01:28,339 --> 00:01:30,573
Spread that over six tires,

41
00:01:30,573 --> 00:01:33,634
and each one carries around a hundred

42
00:01:33,634 --> 00:01:34,213
tonnes.

43
00:01:34,393 --> 00:01:37,311
A small tire would sink into the

44
00:01:37,311 --> 00:01:40,046
haul road, cut itself on rock,

45
00:01:40,046 --> 00:01:41,231
and overheat.

46
00:01:41,411 --> 00:01:44,048
A big tire spreads the weight over

47
00:01:44,048 --> 00:01:45,986
a bigger patch of ground.

48
00:01:46,336 --> 00:01:48,918
The tire size used on the biggest

49
00:01:48,918 --> 00:01:51,735
trucks is called fifty nine eighty R

50
00:01:51,735 --> 00:01:52,675
sixty three.

51
00:01:52,855 --> 00:01:55,425
The last number means the rim is

52
00:01:55,425 --> 00:01:57,513
sixty three inches across,

53
00:01:57,513 --> 00:01:59,681
about one point six meters.

54
00:01:59,861 --> 00:02:02,271
Just the rim is taller than many

55
00:02:02,271 --> 00:02:02,799
people.

56
00:02:02,979 --> 00:02:05,886
Around it sits a rubber casing with

57
00:02:05,886 --> 00:02:07,464
steel cords inside,

58
00:02:07,464 --> 00:02:08,793
built in layers,

59
00:02:08,793 --> 00:02:11,701
so it can bend over rocks thousands

60
00:02:11,701 --> 00:02:14,774
of times a day without tearing apart.

61
00:02:15,125 --> 00:02:17,590
Only a few companies in the world

62
00:02:17,590 --> 00:02:19,158
can make these tires,

63
00:02:19,158 --> 00:02:20,653
among them Michelin,

64
00:02:20,653 --> 00:02:22,520
Bridgestone and Goodyear.

65
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:25,550
Each one takes days to build and

66
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:28,756
cure, and costs tens of thousands of

67
00:02:28,756 --> 00:02:29,469
dollars.

68
00:02:29,649 --> 00:02:31,500
In the mid two thousands,

69
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:32,906
when mining boomed,

70
00:02:32,906 --> 00:02:35,497
the world ran short of giant tires,

71
00:02:35,497 --> 00:02:37,940
and some mines had to park trucks

72
00:02:37,940 --> 00:02:40,680
because they had nothing to put under

73
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:41,050
them.

74
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,056
Why do many mines fill these tires

75
00:02:44,056 --> 00:02:46,322
with nitrogen instead of air?

76
00:02:46,502 --> 00:02:49,428
Because a giant tire can explode.

77
00:02:49,608 --> 00:02:51,618
If the tire gets very hot,

78
00:02:51,618 --> 00:02:53,009
from a brake fire,

79
00:02:53,009 --> 00:02:54,478
a lightning strike,

80
00:02:54,478 --> 00:02:56,797
or someone welding on the rim,

81
00:02:56,797 --> 00:02:59,580
the rubber inside can start to break

82
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:01,589
down into flammable gases.

83
00:03:01,769 --> 00:03:04,511
Mixed with the oxygen in normal air,

84
00:03:04,511 --> 00:03:07,481
those gases can ignite inside the tire.

85
00:03:07,661 --> 00:03:10,589
Nitrogen has no oxygen to feed that

86
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:11,342
reaction.

87
00:03:11,692 --> 00:03:13,909
These explosions are not small.

88
00:03:14,089 --> 00:03:17,185
Mining safety agencies in the United States

89
00:03:17,185 --> 00:03:20,353
and Australia have warned that an overheated

90
00:03:20,353 --> 00:03:23,090
giant tire can burst with enough force

91
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:25,250
to throw the rim and pieces of

92
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:27,194
the truck dozens of meters,

93
00:03:27,194 --> 00:03:29,066
and to kill people nearby.

94
00:03:29,246 --> 00:03:30,234
That is why,

95
00:03:30,234 --> 00:03:32,376
when a tire is overheated,

96
00:03:32,376 --> 00:03:34,764
the rule is to clear the area

97
00:03:34,764 --> 00:03:37,482
and keep people far away for many

98
00:03:37,482 --> 00:03:39,788
hours, sometimes a full day.

99
00:03:40,138 --> 00:03:43,400
Nitrogen also helps the tire itself.

100
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,635
With less oxygen inside,

101
00:03:45,635 --> 00:03:48,632
the rubber and steel cords age more

102
00:03:48,632 --> 00:03:52,228
slowly, and the pressure stays more stable

103
00:03:52,228 --> 00:03:54,797
as the tire heats up and cools

104
00:03:54,797 --> 00:03:55,225
down.

105
00:03:55,405 --> 00:03:57,932
For a tire that costs as much

106
00:03:57,932 --> 00:03:58,717
as a car,

107
00:03:58,717 --> 00:04:01,593
every extra hour of life matters.

108
00:04:01,773 --> 00:04:03,802
It is a simple gas doing a

109
00:04:03,802 --> 00:04:05,128
very serious job.

110
00:04:05,479 --> 00:04:08,236
Why can't a mining truck simply drive

111
00:04:08,236 --> 00:04:10,099
faster to move more rock?

112
00:04:10,279 --> 00:04:12,845
Because the tire would cook itself.

113
00:04:13,025 --> 00:04:15,837
Every time a loaded tire rolls,

114
00:04:15,837 --> 00:04:18,739
the rubber squeezes and relaxes,

115
00:04:18,739 --> 00:04:21,733
and that bending turns into heat.

116
00:04:21,913 --> 00:04:24,252
Rubber is a poor conductor,

117
00:04:24,252 --> 00:04:26,417
so the heat stays inside.

118
00:04:26,597 --> 00:04:27,747
Drive too fast,

119
00:04:27,747 --> 00:04:29,817
or too far without a break,

120
00:04:29,817 --> 00:04:32,194
and the inside of the tire gets

121
00:04:32,194 --> 00:04:35,030
hot enough to separate the layers and

122
00:04:35,030 --> 00:04:35,874
destroy it.

123
00:04:36,224 --> 00:04:39,006
Tire makers give every tire a heat

124
00:04:39,006 --> 00:04:42,772
rating, measured in tonne kilometers per hour.

125
00:04:42,952 --> 00:04:45,390
Mines calculate it for each road.

126
00:04:45,570 --> 00:04:47,217
How heavy is the load,

127
00:04:47,217 --> 00:04:48,714
how far is the trip,

128
00:04:48,714 --> 00:04:50,735
how fast does the truck go,

129
00:04:50,735 --> 00:04:52,756
and how hot is the weather.

130
00:04:52,936 --> 00:04:55,092
If the number is too high,

131
00:04:55,092 --> 00:04:57,662
the mine slows the trucks down,

132
00:04:57,662 --> 00:04:59,238
shortens the route,

133
00:04:59,238 --> 00:05:01,144
or buys tougher rubber.

134
00:05:01,495 --> 00:05:03,416
That is why a truck with a

135
00:05:03,416 --> 00:05:06,965
four thousand horsepower engine usually tops out

136
00:05:06,965 --> 00:05:09,552
at around sixty kilometers an hour.

137
00:05:09,732 --> 00:05:11,520
The limit is not the engine.

138
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:12,652
It is the rubber.

139
00:05:12,832 --> 00:05:15,412
And moving a single new tire to

140
00:05:15,412 --> 00:05:17,410
the mine is a job of its

141
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:20,240
own, often on a special trailer as

142
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:21,655
an oversize load.

143
00:05:22,006 --> 00:05:24,372
Why does this truck have a staircase

144
00:05:24,372 --> 00:05:25,883
built across its front?

145
00:05:26,063 --> 00:05:28,874
Because the driver sits about as high

146
00:05:28,874 --> 00:05:31,230
as the second floor of a house.

147
00:05:31,410 --> 00:05:34,403
A normal ladder would be steep and

148
00:05:34,403 --> 00:05:37,133
dangerous, especially at night,

149
00:05:37,133 --> 00:05:38,541
in rain or snow,

150
00:05:38,541 --> 00:05:39,862
in heavy boots.

151
00:05:40,042 --> 00:05:42,433
So the big trucks have a diagonal

152
00:05:42,433 --> 00:05:44,173
stairway with handrails,

153
00:05:44,173 --> 00:05:47,144
climbing across the front of the radiator

154
00:05:47,144 --> 00:05:49,319
to a walkway that leads to the

155
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:49,608
cab.

156
00:05:49,959 --> 00:05:52,368
Falls are one of the most common

157
00:05:52,368 --> 00:05:54,777
injuries around heavy equipment.

158
00:05:54,957 --> 00:05:57,686
A person climbing down backward,

159
00:05:57,686 --> 00:05:58,965
missing a step,

160
00:05:58,965 --> 00:06:01,865
can fall several meters onto rock.

161
00:06:02,045 --> 00:06:04,378
Stairs let people face forward,

162
00:06:04,378 --> 00:06:06,560
with three points of contact,

163
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,194
and some trucks even have a powered

164
00:06:09,194 --> 00:06:12,129
ladder that lowers itself to the ground

165
00:06:12,129 --> 00:06:14,688
and lifts up when the truck moves.

166
00:06:15,038 --> 00:06:17,487
Most trucks also have a rule.

167
00:06:17,667 --> 00:06:20,105
The driver must not move the truck

168
00:06:20,105 --> 00:06:22,542
until the stairs or ladder are up,

169
00:06:22,542 --> 00:06:24,765
and lights or alarms in the cab

170
00:06:24,765 --> 00:06:26,270
warn if they are not.

171
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:28,306
On a machine this size,

172
00:06:28,306 --> 00:06:31,373
even getting in and out is engineered.

173
00:06:31,723 --> 00:06:34,364
Why does the dump body stretch forward

174
00:06:34,364 --> 00:06:36,726
over the driver's cab like a roof?

175
00:06:36,906 --> 00:06:39,358
Because the truck is loaded by a

176
00:06:39,358 --> 00:06:42,192
shovel that drops dozens of tonnes of

177
00:06:42,192 --> 00:06:43,418
rock from above,

178
00:06:43,418 --> 00:06:45,486
a few bucketfuls at a time.

179
00:06:45,666 --> 00:06:48,420
The operator aims for the middle of

180
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:51,332
the body, but rocks bounce and spill.

181
00:06:51,512 --> 00:06:53,784
A rock the size of a fridge

182
00:06:53,784 --> 00:06:57,401
falling onto an unprotected cab could crush

183
00:06:57,401 --> 00:06:57,654
it.

184
00:06:57,834 --> 00:07:00,353
The canopy takes the hit instead.

185
00:07:00,703 --> 00:07:03,285
This extension is called the canopy,

186
00:07:03,285 --> 00:07:05,221
and it is part of the steel

187
00:07:05,221 --> 00:07:05,939
dump body.

188
00:07:06,119 --> 00:07:08,153
It also protects the engine,

189
00:07:08,153 --> 00:07:10,768
the radiator and the cooling fans at

190
00:07:10,768 --> 00:07:11,494
the front.

191
00:07:11,674 --> 00:07:12,958
On many trucks,

192
00:07:12,958 --> 00:07:16,380
the cab itself has a rollover protective

193
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:19,888
structure, a steel cage tested to survive

194
00:07:19,888 --> 00:07:21,856
if the truck tips over.

195
00:07:22,206 --> 00:07:24,664
The dump body is a wear part

196
00:07:24,664 --> 00:07:25,015
too.

197
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:27,937
Rock sliding out of it scrapes the

198
00:07:27,937 --> 00:07:29,631
steel like sandpaper,

199
00:07:29,631 --> 00:07:31,970
so the floor is made of hard,

200
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:33,664
wear resistant plate,

201
00:07:33,664 --> 00:07:36,487
and some mines add liners of rubber

202
00:07:36,487 --> 00:07:38,907
or steel that can be replaced.

203
00:07:39,087 --> 00:07:41,520
A body can weigh more than thirty

204
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:44,543
tonnes empty, and it still needs repairs.

205
00:07:44,894 --> 00:07:47,932
Why does this diesel truck have electric

206
00:07:47,932 --> 00:07:50,211
motors inside its rear wheels?

207
00:07:50,391 --> 00:07:53,123
Because a normal gearbox has a hard

208
00:07:53,123 --> 00:07:56,324
time handling this much power and weight.

209
00:07:56,504 --> 00:07:59,340
On a diesel electric truck like the

210
00:07:59,340 --> 00:08:01,123
Komatsu nine thirty E,

211
00:08:01,123 --> 00:08:03,959
the diesel engine does not turn the

212
00:08:03,959 --> 00:08:04,526
wheels.

213
00:08:04,706 --> 00:08:06,529
It turns a generator.

214
00:08:06,709 --> 00:08:09,853
The electricity goes through cables to motors

215
00:08:09,853 --> 00:08:11,740
inside the rear wheel hubs,

216
00:08:11,740 --> 00:08:14,186
with gears that multiply the force.

217
00:08:14,536 --> 00:08:17,128
There is no clutch and no gear

218
00:08:17,128 --> 00:08:17,647
shift.

219
00:08:17,827 --> 00:08:19,744
The motors give strong,

220
00:08:19,744 --> 00:08:22,161
smooth force even at a crawl,

221
00:08:22,161 --> 00:08:25,162
which is exactly what a loaded truck

222
00:08:25,162 --> 00:08:27,912
needs when it climbs a steep ramp

223
00:08:27,912 --> 00:08:28,996
out of a pit.

224
00:08:29,176 --> 00:08:30,975
The idea is old.

225
00:08:31,155 --> 00:08:33,065
In the nineteen sixties,

226
00:08:33,065 --> 00:08:35,849
a company called Unit Rig built the

227
00:08:35,849 --> 00:08:38,475
Lectra Haul, one of the first big

228
00:08:38,475 --> 00:08:40,861
diesel electric mining trucks.

229
00:08:41,212 --> 00:08:44,486
Electric drive also helps the brakes.

230
00:08:44,666 --> 00:08:48,642
Going downhill, the motors work in reverse,

231
00:08:48,642 --> 00:08:49,937
as generators.

232
00:08:50,117 --> 00:08:52,619
They slow the truck and turn its

233
00:08:52,619 --> 00:08:55,668
energy into heat in big resistor grids,

234
00:08:55,668 --> 00:08:58,093
so the normal brakes stay cool.

235
00:08:58,273 --> 00:09:00,455
And some mines now go further.

236
00:09:00,635 --> 00:09:03,325
They put overhead power lines on the

237
00:09:03,325 --> 00:09:06,015
uphill ramps, and the trucks raise a

238
00:09:06,015 --> 00:09:07,734
pantograph like a tram,

239
00:09:07,734 --> 00:09:10,573
to climb on electricity from the grid.

240
00:09:10,924 --> 00:09:13,049
Why does the front of a mining

241
00:09:13,049 --> 00:09:15,246
truck look like a giant wall of

242
00:09:15,246 --> 00:09:15,742
grille?

243
00:09:15,922 --> 00:09:18,353
Because behind it is a radiator the

244
00:09:18,353 --> 00:09:19,881
size of a garage door.

245
00:09:20,061 --> 00:09:23,169
The engine in a Caterpillar seven nine

246
00:09:23,169 --> 00:09:25,950
seven F has twenty cylinders and a

247
00:09:25,950 --> 00:09:29,303
displacement of about one hundred and six

248
00:09:29,303 --> 00:09:32,820
liters, roughly fifty times a family car's.

249
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,459
It makes around four thousand horsepower,

250
00:09:36,459 --> 00:09:38,989
and a lot of that energy comes

251
00:09:38,989 --> 00:09:40,002
out as heat,

252
00:09:40,002 --> 00:09:40,930
not motion.

253
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:42,168
In a mine,

254
00:09:42,168 --> 00:09:45,099
that heat has nowhere easy to go.

255
00:09:45,279 --> 00:09:48,055
Trucks work in deserts at more than

256
00:09:48,055 --> 00:09:51,387
forty degrees, climb ramps fully loaded at

257
00:09:51,387 --> 00:09:54,242
low speed, and run around the clock.

258
00:09:54,422 --> 00:09:56,667
A huge fan pulls air through the

259
00:09:56,667 --> 00:09:59,192
radiator, and the whole front of the

260
00:09:59,192 --> 00:10:01,927
truck is shaped around feeding that fan

261
00:10:01,927 --> 00:10:02,909
with cool air.

262
00:10:03,259 --> 00:10:04,942
Dust is the other enemy.

263
00:10:05,122 --> 00:10:06,970
The air is full of fine rock

264
00:10:06,970 --> 00:10:09,941
powder that clogs radiators and wears engines

265
00:10:09,941 --> 00:10:10,997
from the inside.

266
00:10:11,177 --> 00:10:13,907
So the trucks use large air filters,

267
00:10:13,907 --> 00:10:17,397
sometimes with pre-cleaners that spin the dust

268
00:10:17,397 --> 00:10:20,279
out before the air reaches the filter.

269
00:10:20,459 --> 00:10:23,394
Breathing is hard work for a machine

270
00:10:23,394 --> 00:10:24,209
this size.

271
00:10:24,559 --> 00:10:27,266
Why does this truck have sensors and

272
00:10:27,266 --> 00:10:29,748
antennas on a mast above the cab?

273
00:10:29,928 --> 00:10:33,295
Because some mining trucks drive themselves.

274
00:10:33,475 --> 00:10:36,250
The mast carries GPS antennas,

275
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:38,839
radios and obstacle sensors.

276
00:10:39,019 --> 00:10:41,782
A control room tells each truck where

277
00:10:41,782 --> 00:10:44,322
to go, the truck follows a precise

278
00:10:44,322 --> 00:10:45,965
path on the haul road,

279
00:10:45,965 --> 00:10:48,429
and it stops if something gets in

280
00:10:48,429 --> 00:10:49,027
its way.

281
00:10:49,377 --> 00:10:52,854
Autonomous haulage started in real mines around

282
00:10:52,854 --> 00:10:54,259
two thousand eight,

283
00:10:54,259 --> 00:10:56,330
in Chile and in the iron ore

284
00:10:56,330 --> 00:10:58,328
mines of Western Australia.

285
00:10:58,508 --> 00:11:01,240
Today, systems from Komatsu,

286
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:06,021
Caterpillar and others run hundreds of driverless

287
00:11:06,021 --> 00:11:06,705
trucks.

288
00:11:06,885 --> 00:11:09,332
They can work every hour of the

289
00:11:09,332 --> 00:11:12,253
day, without breaks or shift changes,

290
00:11:12,253 --> 00:11:15,411
and the mines report fewer accidents and

291
00:11:15,411 --> 00:11:16,358
lower costs.

292
00:11:16,709 --> 00:11:19,345
Driverless does not mean nobody is in

293
00:11:19,345 --> 00:11:19,843
charge.

294
00:11:20,023 --> 00:11:22,700
People in the control room plan routes

295
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:24,249
and watch every truck.

296
00:11:24,429 --> 00:11:26,326
And the trucks are fenced off from

297
00:11:26,326 --> 00:11:26,717
people.

298
00:11:26,897 --> 00:11:29,974
Anyone who enters the autonomous zone has

299
00:11:29,974 --> 00:11:32,076
to be tracked by the system,

300
00:11:32,076 --> 00:11:34,629
so the trucks know where they are.

301
00:11:34,809 --> 00:11:37,177
The robot still needs humans.

302
00:11:37,357 --> 00:11:39,250
They just sit somewhere else.

303
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:42,787
Why does the BelAZ seven five seven

304
00:11:42,787 --> 00:11:46,066
ten have eight tires instead of six?

305
00:11:46,246 --> 00:11:49,133
Because it is the largest dump truck

306
00:11:49,133 --> 00:11:51,538
ever built, and no tire in the

307
00:11:51,538 --> 00:11:54,104
world is big enough to carry its

308
00:11:54,104 --> 00:11:55,627
load with just six.

309
00:11:55,807 --> 00:11:58,608
Built in Belarus and launched in two

310
00:11:58,608 --> 00:12:01,641
thousand thirteen, it is rated for four

311
00:12:01,641 --> 00:12:03,586
hundred and fifty tonnes.

312
00:12:03,766 --> 00:12:04,543
In a test,

313
00:12:04,543 --> 00:12:07,652
it carried more than five hundred tonnes

314
00:12:07,652 --> 00:12:08,584
in one load.

315
00:12:08,934 --> 00:12:11,768
Instead of inventing a bigger tire,

316
00:12:11,768 --> 00:12:14,925
the engineers used more of the existing

317
00:12:14,925 --> 00:12:17,921
giant ones, two axles with four tires

318
00:12:17,921 --> 00:12:18,326
each.

319
00:12:18,506 --> 00:12:20,048
Two diesel engines,

320
00:12:20,048 --> 00:12:23,619
together making more than four thousand five

321
00:12:23,619 --> 00:12:27,109
hundred horsepower, feed an electric drive.

322
00:12:27,289 --> 00:12:29,233
And unlike most trucks,

323
00:12:29,233 --> 00:12:30,670
both axles steer,

324
00:12:30,670 --> 00:12:32,952
so this giant can turn in a

325
00:12:32,952 --> 00:12:34,220
smaller circle.

326
00:12:34,571 --> 00:12:37,451
It shows a strange truth about mining

327
00:12:37,451 --> 00:12:37,996
trucks.

328
00:12:38,176 --> 00:12:41,073
Their size is not limited by engines

329
00:12:41,073 --> 00:12:41,798
or steel.

330
00:12:41,978 --> 00:12:43,881
It is limited by tires,

331
00:12:43,881 --> 00:12:46,199
by roads, and by the size of

332
00:12:46,199 --> 00:12:48,433
the shovels that load them.

333
00:12:48,613 --> 00:12:50,636
A truck that is too big for

334
00:12:50,636 --> 00:12:53,033
the shovel waits longer to fill,

335
00:12:53,033 --> 00:12:55,729
and that waiting costs more than the

336
00:12:55,729 --> 00:12:57,228
extra rock is worth.

337
00:12:57,578 --> 00:12:59,813
Why can a mining truck driver not

338
00:12:59,813 --> 00:13:01,912
see a car parked right in front

339
00:13:01,912 --> 00:13:02,454
of them?

340
00:13:02,634 --> 00:13:04,742
Because from a cab this high,

341
00:13:04,742 --> 00:13:07,287
the ground near the truck is hidden

342
00:13:07,287 --> 00:13:08,741
by the truck itself.

343
00:13:08,921 --> 00:13:11,054
The blind area in front and on

344
00:13:11,054 --> 00:13:13,330
the right side can be big enough

345
00:13:13,330 --> 00:13:15,747
to hide a pickup truck completely.

346
00:13:15,927 --> 00:13:19,690
Mining safety agencies have recorded many cases

347
00:13:19,690 --> 00:13:23,292
of haul trucks driving over smaller vehicles,

348
00:13:23,292 --> 00:13:25,854
often killing the people inside.

349
00:13:26,204 --> 00:13:29,639
So the mines fight blindness with rules

350
00:13:29,639 --> 00:13:30,697
and gadgets.

351
00:13:30,877 --> 00:13:33,893
Light vehicles carry tall flags on a

352
00:13:33,893 --> 00:13:36,825
pole, sometimes called buggy whips,

353
00:13:36,825 --> 00:13:38,500
with a light on top,

354
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:41,097
so the driver can see them over

355
00:13:41,097 --> 00:13:42,521
the truck's nose.

356
00:13:42,701 --> 00:13:44,396
Trucks have cameras,

357
00:13:44,396 --> 00:13:47,953
mirrors and radar that beep when something

358
00:13:47,953 --> 00:13:48,715
is close.

359
00:13:48,895 --> 00:13:51,751
And small vehicles must ask by radio

360
00:13:51,751 --> 00:13:54,526
before they come near a haul truck.

361
00:13:54,877 --> 00:13:57,779
Another rule is called the berm.

362
00:13:57,959 --> 00:14:00,352
Haul roads have walls of rock along

363
00:14:00,352 --> 00:14:02,403
the edges, at least as tall as

364
00:14:02,403 --> 00:14:03,634
half a truck tire,

365
00:14:03,634 --> 00:14:05,685
so a truck that drifts off the

366
00:14:05,685 --> 00:14:08,078
road hits the berm instead of going

367
00:14:08,078 --> 00:14:09,035
over the edge.

368
00:14:09,215 --> 00:14:10,808
On a machine this size,

369
00:14:10,808 --> 00:14:13,023
safety is not a feature you add.

370
00:14:13,203 --> 00:14:15,885
It is designed into the road too.

371
00:14:16,235 --> 00:14:18,826
Why do some mining trucks send their

372
00:14:18,826 --> 00:14:21,273
hot exhaust through the dump body?

373
00:14:21,453 --> 00:14:23,575
Because in cold climates,

374
00:14:23,575 --> 00:14:26,121
wet rock freezes to the steel.

375
00:14:26,301 --> 00:14:28,499
When the truck tips its body,

376
00:14:28,499 --> 00:14:31,228
part of the load stays stuck inside,

377
00:14:31,228 --> 00:14:32,744
frozen to the floor.

378
00:14:32,924 --> 00:14:34,944
That is called carryback.

379
00:14:35,124 --> 00:14:37,401
The truck drives back to the shovel

380
00:14:37,401 --> 00:14:39,742
still carrying tonnes of rock it was

381
00:14:39,742 --> 00:14:40,848
supposed to dump.

382
00:14:41,198 --> 00:14:43,404
Carryback is pure waste.

383
00:14:43,584 --> 00:14:46,024
The truck burns fuel to haul the

384
00:14:46,024 --> 00:14:47,245
same rock twice,

385
00:14:47,245 --> 00:14:49,380
and it has less room for the

386
00:14:49,380 --> 00:14:50,143
next load.

387
00:14:50,323 --> 00:14:53,673
So trucks working in places like Canada,

388
00:14:53,673 --> 00:14:57,106
Russia and Scandinavia can be fitted with

389
00:14:57,106 --> 00:14:58,195
body heating.

390
00:14:58,375 --> 00:15:01,859
Exhaust gas is routed through channels under

391
00:15:01,859 --> 00:15:03,601
the floor of the body,

392
00:15:03,601 --> 00:15:06,452
warming the steel so the rock slides

393
00:15:06,452 --> 00:15:06,769
out.

394
00:15:07,119 --> 00:15:09,069
It is a clever reuse of heat

395
00:15:09,069 --> 00:15:11,507
the engine would throw away anyway.

396
00:15:11,687 --> 00:15:14,187
And it shows the whole philosophy of

397
00:15:14,187 --> 00:15:15,228
these machines.

398
00:15:15,408 --> 00:15:18,085
When a truck moves hundreds of tonnes

399
00:15:18,085 --> 00:15:19,387
every few minutes,

400
00:15:19,387 --> 00:15:21,846
small losses add up to millions of

401
00:15:21,846 --> 00:15:22,932
dollars a year.

402
00:15:23,112 --> 00:15:25,410
Every part is there to remove one.

403
00:15:25,761 --> 00:15:27,839
So here are the eleven.

404
00:15:28,019 --> 00:15:30,314
A tire taller than a person,

405
00:15:30,314 --> 00:15:33,756
filled with nitrogen so it cannot explode,

406
00:15:33,756 --> 00:15:36,297
with a speed limit set by heat.

407
00:15:36,477 --> 00:15:39,290
A staircase instead of a ladder,

408
00:15:39,290 --> 00:15:41,312
a roof over the driver,

409
00:15:41,312 --> 00:15:44,302
and electric motors in the wheels.

410
00:15:44,482 --> 00:15:47,229
A radiator the size of a garage

411
00:15:47,229 --> 00:15:50,066
door, a mast that lets the truck

412
00:15:50,066 --> 00:15:53,611
drive itself, eight tires on the biggest

413
00:15:53,611 --> 00:15:55,118
truck ever built,

414
00:15:55,118 --> 00:15:57,599
a blind spot that can hide a

415
00:15:57,599 --> 00:16:00,258
car, and a dump body heated by

416
00:16:00,258 --> 00:16:01,676
its own exhaust.

417
00:16:02,027 --> 00:16:04,355
A mining truck looks like a toy

418
00:16:04,355 --> 00:16:06,083
blown up to giant size,

419
00:16:06,083 --> 00:16:07,436
but at this scale,

420
00:16:07,436 --> 00:16:09,839
nothing works the way it does on

421
00:16:09,839 --> 00:16:10,966
a normal truck.

422
00:16:11,146 --> 00:16:14,218
If you want more machines explained part

423
00:16:14,218 --> 00:16:17,137
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

424
00:16:17,317 --> 00:16:19,400
And tell us in the comments which

425
00:16:19,400 --> 00:16:21,483
machine you want us to take apart

426
00:16:21,483 --> 00:16:21,799
next.
